   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">After discussions with Jim Thorson, he identified where the problem is by trying different methods of how the Hessian is calculated. Turns out inside sderport it&#39;s using optimHess which uses finite differences. Here&#39;s a test:<div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><div><br></div><div># Works (using TMB automatic-differentiation hessian</div><div>SD = sdreport( Obj, hessian.fixed=Obj$he(Opt$par) )</div><div><br></div><div># Fails using optimHess for finite-difference hessian using function and gradient</div><div>Hess = optimHess(Opt$par, fn=Obj$fn, gr=Obj$gr)</div><div>SD = sdreport( Obj, hessian.fixed=Hess )</div><div><br></div><div># Fails using optimHess for finite-difference hessian using function only</div><div>Hess = optimHess(Opt$par, fn=Obj$fn )</div><div>SD = sdreport( Obj, hessian.fixed=Hess )</div><div><br></div><div># Also fails using finite-difference hessian</div><div>sqrt(diag(solve(Hess)))</div><div><br></div><div># Works using different finite-difference method</div><div>Hess = numDeriv::hessian( func=Obj$fn, x=Opt$par )</div><div>SD = sdreport( Obj, hessian.fixed=Hess )</div></font></div></code></div><br>Maybe someone has ideas on how to use the AD obj$he function within sdreport (where possible)? Or other general ideas on how to improve it.</div><div><br></div><div>As far as I can tell the core issue is the difference in magnitudes of the SEs of the two parameters. If I divide the covariate by 1000 (or standardize it) the model runs just fine. </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br><br>On Thursday, December 28, 2017 at 6:51:28 PM UTC-3, Cole Monnahan (<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mon...@uw.edu</a>) wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">I’m trying to recreate a simple Poisson GLM (intercept and slope, no random effects) and running into a strange issue. When I fit the model nlminb returns convergence of 1 (false convergence (8)) despite having good gradients. If I do</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">sqrt(diag(solve(Obj$he())))\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">I get the same SEs as the glm function. However, when I run sdreport, with ADREPORT only for the two paramters, I get:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">Warning message:\r\nIn sqrt(diag(cov)) : NaNs produced\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">and then it returns</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">sdreport(.) result\r\n           Estimate Std. Error\r\nbeta0  4.<a href=\"tel:(316)%20484-9854\" value=\"+13164849854\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">3164849854</a>        NaN\r\nbeta  -0.0001058506        NaN\r\nWarning:\r\nHessian of fixed effects was not positive definite.\r\nMaximum gradient component: 0.0002560145 \r\nWarning message:\r\nIn sqrt(diag(object$cov.fixed)) : NaNs produced\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">I’m confused because previously it was able to invert obj$he(). I don’t need to use ADREPORT variables here, obviously, but I’m confused why this would occur. Why would I be able to invert the Hessian directly but sdreport cannot? Further, if I centered the covariate associated with the slope, then TMB fits and both methods work and match the glm function. </p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">I can post the data/model if it helps.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Thanks,<br>Cole</p>\r\n<div title=\"MDH:SSdtIHRyeWluZyB0byByZWNyZWF0ZSBhIHNpbXBsZSBQb2lzc29uIEdMTSAoaW50ZXJjZXB0IGFu\r\nZCBzbG9wZSwgbm8gcmFuZG9tIGVmZmVjdHMpIGFuZCBydW5uaW5nIGludG8gYSBzdHJhbmdlIGlz\r\nc3VlLiBXaGVuIEkgZml0IHRoZSBtb2RlbCBubG1pbmIgcmV0dXJucyBjb252ZXJnZW5jZSBvZiAx\r\nIChmYWxzZSBjb252ZXJnZW5jZSAoOCkpIGRlc3BpdGUgaGF2aW5nIGdvb2QgZ3JhZGllbnRzLiBJ\r\nZiBJIGRvPGRpdj48YnI+PC9kaXY+PGRpdj5gYGBgPC9kaXY+PGRpdj48ZGl2PnNxcnQoZGlhZyhz\r\nb2x2ZShPYmokaGUoKSkpKTwvZGl2PjxkaXY+YGBgYDwvZGl2PjxkaXY+PGJyPjwvZGl2PjxkaXY+\r\nSSBnZXQgdGhlIHNhbWUgU0VzIGFzIHRoZSBnbG0gZnVuY3Rpb24uIEh